Matrix $ A = \begin{pmatrix} a & 2 \\ 1 & 4 \end{pmatrix} $

Matrix $ B = \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{2}{7} & \frac{1}{7} \\ b & \frac{3}{14} \end{pmatrix} $

So, $ AB = I $, where $ I $ is the identity matrix:

$$
I = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}
$$

Now, let me compute the product $ AB $:

$$
AB = \begin{pmatrix} a & 2 \\ 1 & 4 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{2}{7} & \frac{1}{7} \\ b & \frac{3}{14} \end{pmatrix}
$$

To compute this product, I need to multiply the rows of the first matrix by the columns of the second matrix.

Let me recall the multiplication rules:

For two matrices $ \begin{pmatrix} m_{11} & m_{12} \\ m_{21} & m_{22} \end{pmatrix} $ and $ \begin{pmatrix} n_{11} & n_{12} \\ n_{21} & n_{22} \end{pmatrix} $, the product $ AB $ is:

$$
\begin{pmatrix}
m_{11}n_{11} + m_{12}n_{21} & m_{11}n_{12} + m_{12}n_{22} \\
m_{21}n_{11} + m_{22}n_{21} & m_{21}n_{12} + m_{22}n_{22}
\end{pmatrix}
$$

